Flip: The Firewall Log Ingestion Program

DESCRIPTION
Flip is a lightweight program that turns firewall logs such as
ufw.log into human-readable statistics and information while
offering automatic network traffic blocking via UFW.

CONFIGURATION
Flip can be configured with its configuration file, named 'flip.conf'.
Here, you can alter the values of the fields to fit your needs:

1. logLocation: the name of the file to be parsed. Flip will perform an absolute path
check to determine its actual location on the machine, so the value of this variable
should only be the name of the file, such as ufw.log, and NOT /var/log/ufw.log

2. num-blocks-required: the number of blocked network traffic incidents required to 
perform an automatic firewall rule implementation. If the number of blocks required is 0,
then automatic firewall rule implementation will not be used. Otherwise, all IP
addresses recorded in the log file with their respective block counts will be subject to 
being used in a firewall rule denying their incoming traffic.

3. interval: the number of seconds that should elapse between each parses. If your interval
is less than a minute, Fict will generate a warning on screen notifying you about the 
potential downsides to using a short interval. A recommended interval for general use
is no less than 5 minutes unless more frequent firewall log parsing has been advised 
as a security monitoring suggestion.

4. whitelist: a list of all IP address from which network traffic should never 
be blocked. IP addresses in this field should be separated by a comma and
should only be such IPs that you recognize. For example:

whitelist=192.168.50.233, 192.168.9.9

GENERAL USE
1. In the directory you have downloaded Fict in, run the command: “python3 firewall_log_parser.py”. 
Ensure that flip.conf is in the same directory as the script is.
You will be greeted with an introductory message regarding suggestions regarding use.

2. Press the ‘C’ key to continue.

3. Flip will automatically start parsing the firewall log and notify you when it has completed.
If the interval set during configuration is greater than 0 seconds but less than 60 seconds,
Flip will warn you about using a short interval and ask if you would like to continue. 
Otherwise, if the interval is longer, the message will not display and Flip will let you know
that it is waiting the designated interval before the next firewall log parse.

4. After each parse, Flip will generate a human-readable text report titled 'results.rpt' with
the list of all discovered IP addresses in the firewall log and the actions taken on them.
Additionally, a structured JSON report will be generated that provides further details pertaining
to a parsed event such as the source port and destination port of network traffic, as well as the
used network protocol, IP address sending the traffic, and MAC address. Both reports are designed
to be read and used in order to implement any additional security features if needed.

